2026-06-30 15:47:21,933 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-61f2f636-0967-4fb8-856c-17ba50fc41b1', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to sample preparation and digestion.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: cleavage agent details\nsdrf_column: comment[cleavage agent details]\nontology: MS:1001044 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Enzyme or chemical used for protein digestion.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology terms. Multiple enzymes are common in XL-MS.\n  If multiple, return a list.\n  Common terms:\n    Trypsin (MS:1001251) — cleaves K,R (not before P)\n    Lys-C (MS:1001309) — cleaves K\n    Lys-C/P (MS:1001309) — cleaves K including before P\n    Asp-N (MS:1001303) — cleaves N-terminal to D\n    Glu-C (MS:1001917) — cleaves C-terminal to E, D\n    Chymotrypsin (MS:1001306) — cleaves F,Y,W,L\n    No cleavage (MS:1001955)\n    Unspecific cleavage (MS:1001956)\n  Format each as: NT=<enzyme name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: label\nsdrf_column: comment[label]\nontology: PRIDE:0000514, MS\ndescription: Labeling strategy used for quantification.\ninstructions: Most XL-MS is label-free. Use:\n  NT=label free sample;AC=MS:1002038 — for label-free\n  NT=SILAC;AC=MS:1002038 — for SILAC\n  NT=iTRAQ4plex;AC=PRIDE:0000114 — for iTRAQ 4-plex\n  NT=TMT6plex;AC=PRIDE:0000116 — for TMT\n  If not stated, assume label free.\n\nterm: alkylation reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[alkylation reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000598\ndescription: Chemical reagent used for cysteine alkylation (typically before digestion).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Common: iodoacetamide (IAA), iodoacetic acid,\n  N-ethylmaleimide (NEM), acrylamide. If iodoacetamide, use "iodoacetamide".\n\nterm: fractionation method\nsdrf_column: comment[fractionation method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000550\ndescription: Peptide fractionation method used before LC-MS/MS (distinct from crosslink\n  enrichment — this is standard proteomics fractionation).\ninstructions: Return null if no pre-LC fractionation. Examples: "high-pH reversed-phase\n  chromatography", "basic reversed-phase LC", "SCX fractionation", "HILIC".\n\nterm: modification parameters\nsdrf_column: comment[modification parameters]\nontology: MS:1001055, Unimod, PSI-MOD\ndescription: Post-translational modifications searched in the database search.\noutput_format: List of modifications, each formatted as:\n  NT=<mod name>;AC=<Unimod accession>;TA=<target amino acid>;MT=<Fixed or Variable>\ninstructions: Always include carbamidomethyl cysteine if IAA alkylation was used:\n  NT=Carbamidomethyl;AC=UNIMOD:4;TA=C;MT=Fixed\n  Always include oxidation of methionine if searched:\n  NT=Oxidation;AC=UNIMOD:21;TA=M;MT=Variable\n  Include crosslinker mono-link (dead-end) modifications if mentioned.\n  Return a list even if only one modification.\n\nterm: fraction identifier\nsdrf_column: comment[fraction identifier]\nontology: MS:1000858\ndescription: Fraction number for fractionated samples.\ninstructions: Describe whether the dataset uses fractionation and what the fractionation\n  scheme is (e.g. "12 SEC fractions per replicate", "no fractionation").\n  The actual integer values are assigned per-file; describe the scheme here.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nFor comment[modification parameters], "value" should be a list of formatted strings.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nApoD mediates age-associated increase in vulnerability to influenza virus infection\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nSignificance\nAging is a leading risk factor in influenza-related deaths. Additionally, the global population is aging at an unprecedented rate in human history, posing major issues for healthcare and the economy. Thus, there is an urgent need to understand the pathogenesis of severe influenza affecting older patients. Here, we found that highly elevated Apolipoprotein D (ApoD) expression with age drives extensive pulmonary mitophagy to attenuate type I interferon (IFN) response, leading to promotion of influenza virus replication and severe disease outcome. ApoD can therefore be a target for therapeutic intervention to protect against severe influenza virus infection in elderly populations.\nInfluenza A virus (IAV) infection causes significantly greater morbidity and mortality in the elderly population, but the molecular mechanisms in the aging process responsible for severe infection remain unclear. In this study, we found that increased severity in IAV infection and reduced innate immune response correlated with extensive mitophagy in senescent human cells and in the lung of aged mice. Apolipoprotein D (ApoD) was identified as strongly elevated in the lungs and sera of aged human (>65 y old) and mouse (>21 mo old). ApoD was able to localize to mitochondria and interact, through its WXXI motif in the LC3B-Interacting Region domain, with LC3B to trigger mitophagy during IAV infection, in a PINK1 pathway independent manner, which attenuated type I interferon response and promoted virus replication. ApoD deficiency, on the other hand, protected older mice from severe influenza and improved survival. Likewise, depletion of senescent cells by ABT-263, a senolytic compound, in aged mice lowered ApoD level and restored innate immune antiviral response, limiting virus propagation and associated pulmonary damage. Thus, age-induced ApoD drives IAV-mediated mitophagy, and promotes virus replication and infection severity, and is therefore a promising target for inhibition to improve disease outcome in older patients.\n\n[METHODS]\nMaterials and Methods\nApoD−/− C57BL/6 J mice were purchased from the GemPharmatech (Nanjing, China) and bred in Biosafety Level II laboratory (BSL-2). Mice were maintained in specific pathogen-free conditions, and used in challenge experiments. All animal experiments were performed in accordance with institutional guidelines of China Agricultural University (CAU) (approval SKLAB-B-2010-003) and approved by the Beijing Association for Science and Technology of China (approval SYXK, Beijing, 2007-0023).\nDetailed materials and methods are available in SI Appendix.\nData, Materials, and Software Availability\nThe MS proteomics data have been deposited with the ProteomeXchange Consortium via the PRIDE partner repository (https://www.ebi.ac.uk/pride/) with the dataset identifiers PXD057110. All other study data are included in the manuscript and/or SI Appendix.\n\n[RESULTS]\nResults\nSevere IAV Infection in Aged Mice Correlates with Elevated Mitophagy in Pulmonary Epithelial Cells.\nWe first examined senescence status in the lung of mice of contrasting age groups. Aged mice (21 mo old), relative to younger mice (3 mo old), exhibited significantly upregulated cellular senescence marker genes, including the cdkn1a (p21), γ-H2A.X and cdkn2a (p16) (SI Appendix, Fig. S1 A and B), and increased in senescence-associated (SA) β-galactosidase activity (SA-β-gal) (SI Appendix, Fig. S1C). Next, the morbidity and mortality from influenza virus infection (A/PR/8/34, H1N1) of aged and younger mice were ascertained. Consistent with previous findings, aged mice showed elevated viral load in the lung (Fig. 1A) and higher mortality (Fig. 1B) compared with young mice. With regard to body weight, weight loss peaked at 7 d post infection (dpi) in young mice and at 10 dpi in aged mice. Subsequent body weight increases in aged mice was much slower than in young mice. Furthermore, aged mice failed to recover their initial body weight at 22 dpi (Fig. 1C). Severe lung pathology was observed in aged mice at 4 dpi and 6 dpi (Fig. 1D). Additionally, infected aged mice exhibited attenuated messenger RNA (mRNA) expression of type I IFN and ISG15 (SI Appendix, Fig. S1 D and E), and elevated proinflammatory IL6 mRNA (SI Appendix, Fig. S1F).\nWe then determined the effect of influenza virus infection on the mitophagy in aged mouse lungs by analyzing the mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) level and mitochondrial protein expression. By reverse transcription-qPCR (RT-qPCR), mtDNA levels were significantly less in aged mice at 4 dpi and 6 dpi compared with younger mice (Fig. 1E). Immunofluorescence staining for ATP5B, a mitochondrial matrix protein, demonstrated that influenza virus infection reduced the ATP5B expression in young and aged lung tissues. However, reduction was greater in the aged lungs compared with corresponding young mice lung at 6 dpi, suggesting elevated mitophagy in older animals (Fig. 1F). Reduced detection by western blotting of TOMM20, an outer mitochondrial membrane (OMM) protein, and HSP60, a mitochondrial matrix protein, and ATP5B at 4 dpi and 6 dpi (Fig. 1G), further indicated elevated mitophagy in infected lungs of aged mice relative to the younger group. No significant difference in the expression of these mitochondrial proteins was observed at 0 dpi (Fig. 1G), which suggests that basal mitophagy levels were similar between young and aged lungs.\nAn in situ proximity ligation assay (PLA) was developed as a microscopic visualization technique for protein–protein interactions. By in situ PLA, we detected increased colocalization of mitochondria with endogenous LC3B at 4 dpi, as indicated by PLA puncta of LC3B with HSP60 (Fig. 1H), and of LC3B with TOMM20 (SI Appendix, Fig. S1G). To determine lung cell types that were involved in mitophagy and senescence during influenza infection, pulmonary immune cells, endothelial cells, epithelial cells, and interstitial cells were sorted from single cell suspensions of aged lung tissue at 0 dpi and 4 dpi. Western blotting showed reduced ATP5B expression and elevated p16 (senescence marker) and viral PB2 expression in EpCam+ cells after infection (SI Appendix, Fig. S1H). And p16 and EpCam were largely colocalized in the aged lung section at 4 dpi (SI Appendix, Fig. S1I). These results indicated that influenza virus replicated in aged epithelial cells to trigger strong mitophagy. When we used Mdivi-1, an inhibitor of mitochondrial fission, to treat aged mice, we found that IAV-induced mitophagy in aged lung tissues could be blocked by Mdivi-1, as assessed by mtDNA levels (SI Appendix, Fig. S1J). However, there were no significant differences observed for the expression of IFN-β and ISG15 (SI Appendix, Fig. S1 K and L) and viral loads (SI Appendix, Fig. S1M) in Mdivi-1 treated aged and young lung tissues at 5 dpi. Together, these findings indicate that influenza virus infection in the aged lung induces elevated levels of mitophagy in pulmonary epithelial cells, attenuated innate antiviral immune response and elevated viral replication.\nNext, cellular senescence in alveolar type II-like epithelial (A549) cells was induced with bleomycin. A549 cells exposed to 50 μg/mL of bleomycin for 72 h resulted in typical phenotypic senescence, characterized by increase in SA-β-gal activity (SI Appendix, Fig. S2A), distinct enlarged morphology (SI Appendix, Fig. S2B), and significantly elevated p16 mRNA expression (SI Appendix, Fig. S2C). The bleomycin-induced A549 cells were used to mimic age-mediated senescence. Bleomycin-treated or control A549 cells were infected with influenza virus, and the results showed that bleomycin-induced cellular senescence further increased mitophagy during infection, as determined by elevated LC3B expression and degradation of the inner mitochondrial membrane (IMM) protein TIM23 and HSP60 (SI Appendix, Fig. S2D), and by reduced immunofluorescence of ATP5B puncta (SI Appendix, Fig. S2E). Additionally, in situ PLA showed increased colocalization of LC3B and HSP60 in the bleomycin-induced A549 cells post infection, as determined by quantitative image analysis of PLA spots at 18 hpi (SI Appendix, Fig. S2F). Western blotting indicated elevated detection of viral PB2 protein in bleomycin-induced A549 cells post infection. Mdivi-1 treatment reduced the difference of PB2 expression between control and bleomycin-induced senescent cells at 12 and 24 hpi (SI Appendix, Fig. S2G). There were also no significant differences observed for the expression of IFN-β and ISG15 in Mdivi-1 treated bleomycin-induced and control A549 cells at 12 hpi (SI Appendix, Fig. S2 H and I). Notably, bleomycin-induced cellular senescence in IFNAR1-KO A549 cells did not increase viral replication (SI Appendix, Fig. S2J). Thus, these results reveal that increased viral replication in senescent cells is attributed to enhanced mitophagy-mediated attenuation of the innate antiviral immune response.\nAge-Induced ApoD Coincides with Mitophagy during Influenza Virus Infection.\nWe hypothesized that an age-related host factor could be responsible for enhanced mitophagy in senescent cells during influenza virus infection. Recruitment of autophagosomal membrane-associated protein LC3 to mitochondria is a key step in driving mitophagy. To screen age-related regulators of mitophagy, we examined LC3B-interacting proteins by proximity-dependent biotin identification. LC3B was cloned into a mammalian expression vector tagged with a mitochondrial OMM-targeted APEX2 (mito-APEX) label for proximity-based identification of LC3B-interacting proteins localized or trafficked to mitochondria during influenza virus infection (Fig. 2A). Bleomycin-induced senescent A549 cells, transfected with the mito-APEX-tagged LC3B plasmid for 18 h, revealed mitochondrial localization of LC3B (SI Appendix, Fig. S3A). Following 18 hpi and biotin affinity capture, biotinylated proteins were purified and analyzed by mass spectrometry to identify LC3B-interacting proteins (Fig. 2A). A total of 20 candidate proteins were identified as LC3B-binding partners (Fig. 2B). Further investigation into the expression of corresponding genes in bleomycin-induced senescent A549 cells during infection identified ApoD as one of the most highly expressed candidates (Fig. 2C).\nIAV infection increased the interaction between ApoD and LC3B in bleomycin-induced senescent A549 cells, as indicated by biotinylation of ApoD in mito-APEX-LC3B system (Fig. 2D), and coimmunoprecipitation of ApoD with the mito-APEX-LC3B in the absence and presence of IAV infection (SI Appendix, Fig. S3B). In addition, the AirID system, another proximity-based biotin labeling method, was used to examine protein interactions with ApoD which showed that IAV infection promoted the association of ApoD with mitochondria in bleomycin-induced senescent A549 cells (SI Appendix, Fig. S3 C and D). IAV infection also enhanced the colocalization of exogenous ApoD with TOMM20 (SI Appendix, Fig. S3E) and of endogenous ApoD with LC3B (SI Appendix, Fig. S3F) in A549 cells, indicating that viral infection induces the mitochondrial localization of ApoD and its interaction with LC3B. To further confirm whether cellular senescence promoted the binding of ApoD to LC3B in mitochondria during IAV infection, in situ PLAs were conducted which indicated elevated number of ApoD and HSP60 puncta in bleomycin-induced senescent A549 cells at 18 hpi relative to corresponding control cells (Fig. 2 E and F). Additionally, increased colocalization of endogenous ApoD and LC3B was observed in senescent cells after infection (Fig. 2 G and H). These findings demonstrate that the increased mitochondrial localization of ApoD facilitates its binding to LC3B in senescent cells, and promotion of mitophagy during IAV infection.\nWe next assessed ApoD expression in normal lung tissues of five elderly donors (>65 y) and five younger adults (<40 y) by immunohistochemical analysis which showed strong detection of ApoD in a large number of cells from older lungs relative to corresponding younger adult lungs (Fig. 2 I and J). Additionally, elderly patients with influenza exhibited significantly higher levels of ApoD in serum compared with younger patients (Fig. 2K). Furthermore, confocal fluorescence analysis of the human lung section revealed highly expression of ApoD in EpCam+ cells from an elderly donor when compared with the young donor (SI Appendix, Fig. S4A). Distinct colocalization of ApoD with surfactant protein-C (SFTPC) and less coexpression between ApoD and homeodomain-only protein homeobox (HOPX) was also observed in lung section from an elderly donor (SI Appendix, Fig. S4B), which suggests that the pulmonary epithelial cells coexpressing ApoD were mainly alveolar type 2 epithelial cells. Similarly elevated expression of ApoD in the lungs and sera of aged mice (mock and infected) was detected (SI Appendix, Fig. S4 C–G). However, highly expressed ApoD induced by IAV in mouse lungs was not limited to bronchial epithelial cells (SI Appendix, Fig. S4C). ApoD was also significantly induced in bleomycin-induced senescent A549 cells at mock or infection condition when compared with that in control cells (SI Appendix, Fig. S4H). Together, these results indicate that ApoD expression is elevated in the lungs and sera of aged human and mouse, and that ApoD localizes to mitochondria and interacts with autophagosome-associated protein LC3 during IAV infection.\nApoD Is Required for Influenza Virus-Induced Mitophagy.\nNext, we found that use of ApoD−/− HEK293 cells significantly prevented IAV-induced degradation of mitochondrial proteins, as demonstrated by fewer ATP5B puncta detected through immunofluorescence (Fig. 3A), and by reduced IMM protein TIM23 degradation (SI Appendix, Fig. S5A). Moreover, absence of ApoD abrogated virus-induced reduction of mtDNA levels (SI Appendix, Fig. S5B). Mitochondrial transmembrane potential was assessed using tetramethylrhodamine, methyl ester (TMRE) staining, with use of CCCP as a positive control for membrane potential reduction. At 24 hpi, significant decrease in mitochondrial membrane potential was observed in wild-type HEK293 cells, similar to CCCP treatment (Fig. 3B). However, ApoD deficiency restored mitochondrial membrane potential. Electron microscopy was used to visualize lysosomal mitochondrial degradation. In wild-type cells, mitochondria were enclosed by autophagosomes or autolysosomes postinfluenza virus infection or CCCP treatment. Conversely, in ApoD−/− HEK293 cells, numerous morphologically normal mitochondria were observed, indicating absence of significant mitochondrial degradation (Fig. 3C). Additionally, in Bleomycin-induced senescent A549 cells depleted of ApoD expression by siRNA transfection, mitochondrial localization of LC3B and ApoD was significantly reduced, as measured by colocalization of endogenous LC3B or ApoD with HSP60 via in situ PLAs (Fig. 3D and SI Appendix, Fig. S5C). These findings establish a critical role for ApoD in influenza virus-induced mitophagy.\nThe LC3B-Interacting Region (LIR) Domain of ApoD Is Essential for Interaction with LC3B and for Mitophagy.\nWe next assessed the functional importance of the LIR domain in ApoD in mitophagy. Amino acid sequence in ApoD harbors a highly conserved LIR motif, as predicted by the iLIR web server (Fig. 4A). Comparative coimmunoprecipitation assays using WT ApoD and a panel of ApoD mutants at the LIR domain (as single mutation, double mutations, and complete deletion) demonstrated that the LIR domain is essential for binding to LC3B during IAV infection (SI Appendix, Fig. S6A). Single and double-amino acid substitutions in the LIR domain significantly reduced the degradation of mitochondrial HSP60 protein triggered by influenza virus infection (SI Appendix, Fig. S6B).\nNext, we transfected separately WT ApoD and LIR-depleted ApoD mutant in ApoD−/− HEK293 cells followed by influenza virus (PR8) infection. Overexpression of WT ApoD effectively promoted the degradation of mitochondrial TOMM20 protein (SI Appendix, Fig. S6C) and significantly reduced detection of mtDNA (Fig. 4B), while the LIR-depleted ApoD mutant in ApoD−/− HEK293 cells did not promote mitochondrial degradation. Consistently, WT ApoD strongly colocalized with LC3B, indicating that the LIR motif is essential for its interaction during influenza virus infection (Fig. 4C); similar results were obtained by in situ PLAs (Fig. 4 D and E). Therefore, the LIR domain of ApoD is essential for binding to LC3B and plays a regulatory role in influenza virus-induced mitophagy.\nApoD Regulates Influenza Virus-Induced Mitophagy in a PINK1-Independent Manner.\nPINK1-Parkin-mediated mitophagy is a main pathway for eliminating damaged mitochondria, induced by various stimuli such as reactive oxygen species (ROS), CCCP, and infection. To determine whether ApoD-regulated mitophagy is dependent on the PINK1-Parkin pathway, expression of PINK1 and Parkin genes in PR8 virus-infected ApoD−/− HEK293 cells was assessed by RT-PCR. Parkin mRNA, but not PINK1 mRNA, was induced by influenza virus infection but this induction was unaffected by ApoD deficiency (SI Appendix, Fig. S6 D and E). While CCCP treatment effectively induced PINK1-dependent mitophagy, it did not alter the expression of ApoD protein (SI Appendix, Fig. S6F). ApoD overexpression in HEK293 cells did not promote CCCP-induced mitophagy (SI Appendix, Fig. S6G). Although CCCP was previously shown to induce ubiquitination of mitochondria in Parkin overexpressing cells, we did not observe significant change in ubiquitination of mitochondria in the ApoD−/− HEK293 cells during influenza virus infection (SI Appendix, Fig. S6H), hence excluding the involvement of ubiquitination in the regulation underlying the functions. Additionally, PINK1 knockout did not affect the elevated mitophagy induced by ApoD overexpression in HEK293 cells (Fig. 4 F and G). ApoD overexpression had no impact on basal and starvation-induced levels of LC3B-II (SI Appendix, Fig. S6 I and J). However, ApoD overexpression in cells depleted of ATG5, an important factor in the formation of autophagy vesicles, failed to promote mitophagy during influenza virus infection (SI Appendix, Fig. S6K). Collectively, our data suggest that ApoD is a binding partner of LC3 that regulates mitophagy induced by influenza virus independent of the PINK1 pathway.\nApoD Negatively Regulates the Innate Immune Response.\nTo determine the impact of elevated ApoD levels on influenza virus dynamics, we transfected ApoD-HA in HEK293 cells followed by PR8 virus infection. Overexpression of ApoD significantly increased virus titers at 12 and 24 hpi (SI Appendix, Fig. S7A) and enhanced the protein expression of viral NP and NS1 (SI Appendix, Fig. S7B). In contrast, knocking down ApoD using siRNA resulted in significant decrease in virus titers in A549 cells (SI Appendix, Fig. S7C) and viral proteins in HEK293 cells and A549 cells (SI Appendix, Fig. S7 D and E). We also used other influenza subtypes, avian H9N2, H5N1, and H7N9 viruses, to show that ApoD overexpression in HEK293 cells similarly increased protein expression of viral NP and NS1 (SI Appendix, Fig. S7 F–H). To investigate the role of endogenous ApoD during IAV infection, we infected ApoD+/+ and ApoD−/− HEK293 cells with PR8 virus. Infected ApoD−/− HEK293 cells produced significantly lower virus titers at 12 and 24 hpi (Fig. 5A) and exhibited reduced protein levels of viral NP and PB2 at 6, 12, and 24 hpi (Fig. 5B). Additionally, influenza virus entry, as assessed by GFP detection at 18 hpi using recombinant PR8 expressing GFP (PR8-GFP), was reduced in ApoD−/− HEK293 cells as determined by flow cytometry and immunofluorescence (SI Appendix, Figs. S7I and S8A). Moreover, mitophagy inhibitor Mdivi-1 blocked ApoD-mediated promotion of viral replication (SI Appendix, Fig. S8B). A similar finding was made with ATG5 KO HEK293 cells (SI Appendix, Fig. S8 C and D).\nGiven that ApoD facilitated virus replication in the promotion of mitophagy, we investigated its impact on innate immune response. Overexpression of ApoD significantly reduced PR8-virus-induced mRNA expression of IFN-β, IFN-α, and ISG15 at 12 hpi (SI Appendix, Fig. S8 E–G). Conversely, ApoD deficiency resulted in substantial increase in PR8-virus-induced expression of IFN-β, IFN-α, and ISG15 at 12 hpi in A549 cells (SI Appendix, Fig. S8 H–J). Furthermore, ApoD deficiency increased PR8-virus-induced IFN-β protein production in a time-dependent manner at 12, 18, 24, and 36 hpi (Fig. 5C). Consistent with such findings, ApoD deficiency in infected cells was associated with increased detection of phosphorylated IRF3 compared with the ApoD+/+ group (Fig. 5 D and E). Overexpression of ApoD failed to increase the viral protein expression in IFNAR1−/− A549 cells, indicating that ApoD facilitates virus replication by attenuating innate immune response (SI Appendix, Fig. S8K). Thus, ApoD serves as a potent negative regulator of type I IFN response to favor influenza virus replication.\nNext, the impact of elevated ApoD expression in bleomycin-induced senescent cells on type I IFN response was assessed during influenza virus infection. Knockdown of highly expressed ApoD in senescent A549 cells by siRNA transfection (SI Appendix, Fig. S8L) significantly reduced progeny virus titers at 24 hpi (Fig. 5F). Notably, the knockdown of ApoD restored type I IFN response, as determined by RT-qPCR, with increased expression of IFN-β, IFN-α, and ISG15 mRNA (Fig. 5 G and H and SI Appendix, Fig. S8M). Additionally, reduced ApoD expression was associated with decreased expression of IL-6 (SI Appendix, Fig. S8N). Taken together, ApoD functions as a negative regulator of type I IFN response during influenza virus infection.\nApoD Deficiency Protects Older Mice from Severe Influenza.\nOur findings so far indicate that elevated ApoD expression is associated with severe influenza virus infection in elderly humans and aged mice. An ApoD-deficient (ApoD−/−) mouse model was used to investigate the in vivo role of ApoD in influenza virus infection (SI Appendix, Fig. S9 A–C). ApoD deficiency did not change the basal senescence state of the aged mice (SI Appendix, Fig. S9D). ApoD−/− and WT young and aged mice were each intranasally (i.n.) infected with a severe dose of PR8 virus. Virus infection significantly induced ApoD expression in WT aged mice compared with young mice (Fig. 6A). All infected young ApoD−/− mice survived; 60% of infected young WT mice survived (Fig. 6B). Infected young ApoD−/− mice showed no significant body weight loss compared with WT counterparts (Fig. 6C).\nWhile ~70% of ApoD−/− aged mice survived, all WT aged mice succumbed to infection by 8 dpi (Fig. 6B). WT aged mice showed significantly greater weight loss compared with ApoD−/− aged mice at 7 and 8 dpi (P < 0.05) (Fig. 6C). Virus load in the lungs of ApoD−/− aged mice at 5 dpi was significantly less than that of corresponding WT mice (Fig. 6D). Immuno-detection of viral NP further confirmed higher progeny virus load in WT aged animals compared with ApoD−/− aged mice (SI Appendix, Fig. S9E). There was no notable difference in virus distribution at the bronchial and alveolar epithelia between ApoD−/− and WT mice regardless of age. Moreover, immunofluorescence staining for viral NP and ApoD protein in lung sections of aged and young WT mice showed spatial coincidence of influenza virus with ApoD protein (Fig. 6E).\nGross pathology and histopathology of PR8-infected WT aged mice (relative to younger mice) showed more severe lung damage at 5 dpi (SI Appendix, Fig. S9F), characterized by acute necrotizing bronchitis, disruption of the respiratory epithelium, multifocal lymphocytic infiltration of the interstitium, vasculitis, and areas of high-protein edema suggestive of viral pneumonia (Fig. 6 F and G). In ApoD−/− aged mice, however, pulmonary pathology was less severe (Fig. 6 F and G). Young ApoD−/− and WT mice showed comparably mild pathological changes 5 dpi. The lung of PR8-infected ApoD−/− aged mice showed significant reduction in mitophagy relative to corresponding WT mice at 5 dpi as determined by the colocalization of LC3B and HSP60 on the lung sections via PLA (Fig. 6 H and I) and western blotting of mitochondrial HSP60 and ATP5B proteins (SI Appendix, Fig. S9 G and H). Impaired type I IFN response in WT aged mice was restored in ApoD−/− aged mice during virus infection (Fig. 6J and SI Appendix, Fig. S9I). Moreover, ApoD deficiency in aged mice also significantly elevated IFN-β and ISG15 expression at 4 d post poly(I:C) stimulation (SI Appendix, Fig. S9 J–L), indicating that ApoD is acting on innate immune repression. Overall, our data indicate that ApoD is a driver of severe influenza disease in aged mice.\nABT-263 Lowers ApoD Expression and Reduces Disease Severity in Aged Mice.\nSenolytics represent an emerging class of drugs that can selectively eliminate senescent cells. ABT-263, a potent senolytic that targets antiapoptotic pathways to deplete senescent cells, has recently shown to have therapeutic potential against SARS-CoV-2 infection in aged hamsters. ABT-263 administration for seven consecutive days reduced p16-positive cells in lung tissues from aged mice (SI Appendix, Fig. S10 A and B). Notably, ABT-263 treatment significantly reduced ApoD expression in uninfected aged mice (SI Appendix, Fig. S10 C and D). Influenza virus infection can induce cellular senescence in young animals. Intriguingly, influenza infection-induced lung cell senescence was exacerbated in aged mice compared with their young counterparts, as assessed by p16 expression (SI Appendix, Fig. S10 E and F).\nABT-263 was administered to young and aged mice via oral gavage for seven consecutive days before influenza infection and then daily until they were killed. Treatment with ABT-263 significantly reduced the p16-positive cells in lung tissues of aged mice at 5 dpi (Fig. 7A). In contrast, ABT-263 treatment failed to decrease the frequency of p16-positive cells in infected young mice at 5 dpi (SI Appendix, Fig. S11A). Furthermore, ABT-263 treatment reduced expression of ApoD in infected, aged lungs at 5 dpi (Fig. 7B). Five out of six ABT-263-treated infected mice survived, while all but one aged mouse in the DMSO control group died (Fig. 7C). Aged control mice significantly lost more body weight compared with ABT-263-treated mice (P < 0.05 on days 4, 5, and 7) (SI Appendix, Fig. S11B). Virus load in the lungs of ABT-263-treated aged mice was significantly reduced at 5 dpi (Fig. 7D). Immunohistochemical detection of viral NP was noticeably stronger in lung sections of control aged mice (SI Appendix, Fig. S11C). Infected lungs from ABT-263-treated aged mice showed reduced pulmonary pathology relative to control aged mice (Fig. 7E and SI Appendix, Fig. S11D). However, ABT-263 treatment was less effective in ApoD−/− aged mice (SI Appendix, Fig. S11E) or young mice (SI Appendix, Fig. S11 F–H) at reducing viral loads in lungs and pulmonary pathology. The less severe infection outcome in ABT-263-treated aged mice was associated with elevated type I IFN response (Fig. 7F), attributed to the inhibition of mitophagy induced by influenza virus infection (Fig. 7G and SI Appendix, Fig. S11I). In summary, depletion of senescent cells by ABT-263 in aged mice lowers ApoD levels and restores innate immune antiviral response, thereby limiting influenza virus propagation and associated pulmonary damage.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
